  "summary": "Benchmarks of larger pretrained and classical models across ADME, toxicity, and PAMPA datasets question whether scale improves molecular prediction.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is how molecular and peptide sequences are encoded and how much physicochemical structure those encodings retain. A 26-endpoint benchmark across random and scaffold splits finds classical ML often matches or exceeds larger pretrained sequence and GNN models, and a 143-molecule multitask PAMPA study over six membranes finds physicochemical descriptors outperform deep representations. Other items apply pretrained protein language model embeddings: PALM uses them to predict peptide aggregation via transfer learning, and a plant food allergen predictor uses ProtTrans embeddings. A conditional VAE with a LoRA-tuned ProtGPT2 decoder generates targeted antimicrobial peptides under QSAR-guided surrogate weighting. What is new is the direct head-to-head evidence that representation choice, not model scale, governs predictive performance; the remaining items do not benchmark against deep models, so the shared claim is limited to representation affecting outcomes.",
